DERIVED — published algorithms, independently implemented
================================================================================

No third-party code was copied for any of the following. Each is bldr's own
implementation of a published design, credited to its authors.

BLAKE3 SIMD kernels
  source/infrastructure/utils/simd/c/blake3_{avx2,avx512,sse2,sse41,neon}.c
  source/infrastructure/utils/simd/c/blake3_dispatch.c
  Written against the BLAKE3 specification (O'Connor, Aumasson, Neves,
  Wilcox-O'Hearn, 2020) rather than transliterated from upstream's kernels.
  The IV and message-schedule constants are from the specification.
  https://github.com/BLAKE3-team/BLAKE3-specs

Chase-Lev work-stealing deque
  source/infrastructure/utils/concurrency/deque.d
  Chase, D. and Lev, Y. "Dynamic Circular Work-Stealing Deque." SPAA 2005.
  Memory ordering follows Lê, N. M., Pop, A., Cohen, A., and Zappa Nardelli, F.
  "Correct and Efficient Work-Stealing for Weak Memory Models." PPoPP 2013.

FastCDC content-defined chunking / Gear rolling hash
  source/infrastructure/utils/files/cdc.d
  source/infrastructure/utils/simd/gear.d
  Xia, W. et al. "FastCDC: A Fast and Efficient Content-Defined Chunking
  Approach for Data Deduplication." USENIX ATC 2016.

Jump consistent hashing
  source/engine/distributed/coordinator/hash.d
  Lamping, J. and Veach, E. "A Fast, Minimal Memory, Consistent Hash
  Algorithm." Google, 2014. arXiv:1406.2294

Bloom filters
  source/infrastructure/utils/simd/c/bloom.c, bloom_simd.c
  Bloom, B. H. "Space/Time Trade-offs in Hash Coding with Allowable Errors."
  CACM 13(7), 1970.

Merkle trees
  source/infrastructure/utils/crypto/merkle.d
  Merkle, R. C. "A Digital Signature Based on a Conventional Encryption
  Function." CRYPTO 1987.

================================================================================
DESIGN INFLUENCE
================================================================================

No code or specification is shared with these projects. They shaped bldr's
design and are named because the resemblance is deliberate, not accidental.

  Bazel        Toolchain resolution model; `bldr query` follows Bazel's query
               language so existing queries transfer. Target label syntax
               (//path/to:target) is Bazel's.
  Rust         Target triple format used by the cross-compilation layer.
  LLVM         Platform and target abstractions.
  Zig          Cross-compilation model — toolchains as data, not as a
               separately installed cross-compiler per target.
  Buck2        Dynamic build graphs, where actions discover dependencies at
               runtime instead of requiring a code-generation pass.

Further detail: source/infrastructure/toolchain/README.md

